$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Carboxylic acid 22 (1.08 kg, 4.78 mol) was dissolved in THF (11 L) and
1,1′-carbonyldiimidazole (1.163 kg, 7.17 mol) was charged at 20- 25 °C
over 10 minutes. The mixture was heated to 65 °C (caution: CO2 gas
evolution) for 1 h, at which point TLC analysis (nheptane/EtOAc 1:1,
ninhydrin) indicated the formation of the activated ester (Rf 0.3). The
reaction was cooled to -10 °C and 1-methylcyclopropane-1-sulfonamide (17;
977 g, 7.22 mol) was charged to the mixture over 10 min, maintaining the
temperature between -10 and -2 °C. The reaction mixture was allowed to
warm to 20-25 °C prior to the steady addition of
1,8-diazabicyclo[5.4.0]undec-7-ene (1.455 kg, 9.56 mol), which was
performed over 30 min between 20 and 29 °C. The reaction mixture was
stirred for 16 h at 20-25 °C. The solvent was distilled at 40-45 °C to
give a brown oil, which was then dissolved in DCM (4 L) and washed with
NaHCO3 (aq, saturated, 2 × 4 L), followed by HCl (aq, 1 N, 2 × 4 L). The
organic phase was dried using anhydrous Na2SO4, filtered under vacuum and
distilled at 45 °C to give sulfonamide 23 crude yellow solid (1.576 kg,
95%).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM safety (CO2 gas evolved)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Development of Related HCV Protease Inhibitors: Macrocyclization of Two
Highly Functionalized Dienyl-ureas via Ring-Closing Metathesis
